      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2011 International Society for Sexual Medicine.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "The increased prevalence of erectile dysfunction (ED) has been reported in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and sustained systemic inflammation seems to play a central role in this linkage. Asthma is also a chronic inflammatory airway disorder, eliciting a low-grade systemic inflammation; however, the influence of asthma on ED has not been investigated."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIM",
            "content": "Our study strived to explore the relationship of asthma and the subsequent development of ED using a nationwide, population-based database."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "From 2000 to 2007, we identified newly diagnosed asthma cases involving male patients 18-55 years old. A control cohort without asthma, which was matched for age and comorbidities, was selected for comparison."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "The two cohorts were followed up, and we observed the occurrence of ED by registry of ED diagnosis in the database."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Of the 17,302 sampled patients (3,466 asthma patients vs. 13,836 control), 114 (0.66%) experienced ED during a mean follow-up period of 4.56 years, including 34 (0.98% of the asthma patients) from the asthma cohort and 80 (0.58%) from the control group. Subjects with asthma experienced a 1.909-fold (95% confidence interval [CI], 1.276-2.856; P=0.002) increase in incident ED, which was independent of age, the number of clinical visits for urologist, and other comorbidities. Kaplan-Meier analysis also revealed the tendency of asthma patients for ED development (log rank test, P=0.002). The risk of ED was higher in cases with more frequent clinical visits for asthma (asthma patients with clinical visits with >24 times/year vs. <12 times/year: hazard ratio [HR]: 4.154 [95% CI:1.392-12.396], P=0.011; clinical visits with 12-24 times/year vs. <12 times/year HR: 3.534 [95% CI:1.245-10.032], P=0.018)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Asthma may be an independent risk factor for ED, and risk of ED probably increases in accordance with asthma severity."
          }
        ]
      },
